# Copy to .env.development (local) or .env.production (build for deploy).
# Both are gitignored; only this template is committed.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING ANYTHING:
#
# Vite inlines every VITE_* variable into the JavaScript bundle at BUILD time.
# The values ship to the browser in plain text and anyone can read them with
# devtools. They are configuration, not secrets, and .gitignore does not change
# that — it only keeps them out of the repo.
#
# So: never put an API secret, private key, or database credential here, no
# matter how well the file is protected. Anything genuinely secret belongs in
# the .NET API's environment (deploy/.env on the server) and must be reached
# through an API call, never held by the frontend.
#
# This also means Cloudflare Worker secrets (`wrangler secret put`) cannot
# configure these values — the bundle is already built by then. To change one,
# edit the file and redeploy.
#
# A Google OAuth *client ID* is public by design, so it is fine here. A Google
# client *secret* is not, and never goes in a frontend build.

# Base URL of the .NET API, including the /api suffix — src/config/env.tsx
# expects it and derives the SignalR hub URL (/hubs/crypto) by stripping it.
#   local:      http://localhost:5000/api
#   production: https://your-api-hostname/api
#
# vite.config.ts rejects a production build whose value is missing, non-https,
# localhost, or lacking the /api suffix.
VITE_API_BASE_URL="http://localhost:5000/api"

# Public OAuth client ID. Remember to add your deployed origin to the client's
# "Authorized JavaScript origins" in the Google console, or sign-in works
# locally and fails in production.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID"
